h3. {color:#008da6}Updated the process map with up-to-date info, reviewed Sintija’s insights, and added mine. {color}

* *Cancellation root causes* - what are they? What can we do to prevent tickets? Get insights from Supportability.
* *Investigation step sequence* - the way the investigation steps are explained currently tells the agent what to check, but doesn't specify what exactly to look for. Need to figure out the most suitable layout for the investigation steps to help the agent understand what they’re looking for exactly, also keeping in mind that in the future investigation will be automated.
** _(missing step)_ Investigation should start with checking who cancelled the ride and why (together with the rider’s report, it provides fuller context and direction for what situation to validate during the investigation)
** _(missing step)_ Next, agent should check if there was a cancellation fee, to know if they should follow the next steps.
** _(unclear step)_ What should the agent look for when checking the driver’s route to the rider’s pickup point? Most likely this information is relevant when the rider cancelled the ride before the driver arrived (to validate if the driver moved towards the rider’s pickup point, took a weird route, etc).
** _(unclear step)_ How should the agent determine if the pickup point was safe to reach/arrive at? Should they check Google Maps?
** _(unclear step)_ Need to clarify what to do after checking the search algorithm (B2B or normal). 
** _(unclear step)_ Need to clarify what to do after checking if it was a scheduled ride.
* *Cancellation fee in cash rides* - find out how that works. Bolt Balance debt? 
** “Customer has an old in-app payment” scenario - can a refund be done to the original payment method?
** “The rider will still be able to order rides as long as they don't have two accumulated negative balances." - what does it mean?
* *Redundant steps* - 
** If R reports an unknown charge, follow a relevant process - common knowledge;
** If R travels with kids, advise to use a special category - is this a relevant step often? Seems like this step is required for edge cases;
** (if this was their first trip) Refund the small sum value to R’s - follow up with Ayush Jain and see if their project went live _“We are working on an experiment in which we intend to not charge the Cancellation Fee to the new users on the first (or maybe second too) order”_. If yes, this step is redundant;
** Don’t offer compensation for their consecutive trips - something that shouldn't be done can't be a step in the process;
** Check if automation applied - should be mentioned only once, no need to add it to each scenario;
** Select the correct order from Bolt Admin Panel - redundant;
* *Leaving a comment* - current process asks to leave a comment _“Customer complaints, cancellation fee overcharge”_ on driver’s profile if the fee was charged incorrectly. Quality team suggested specifying in the process when exactly the comment is needed ([more info here|https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/CMO-9878]). I could try that with this process and then see if it would work for other processes too.
* *Additional info about cancellation fee* - I don’t agree with Sintija’s idea to have a separate informative article about cancellation fee, I think it’s better to have all info in one article and avoid agents searching for info through different articles. But I see how all that info could be edited:
** When are cancellation fees applicable (and when not) - could be a dropdown after a step to check if cancellation fee applied.
** Checking cancellation fees setup - double check if nothing changed in the Admin panel; check if that’s a part of the training; add info on how to check the local market’s cancellation fee setup (country information articles).

