Defining edges and nodes is easy enough:

{noformat}const [graphNodes, graphEdges] = React.useMemo(() => {
    return [
        [
            {id: "start", text: "Start"},
            {id: "wait", text: "Waiting 3h"},
            {id: "enroll_and_wait", text: "Enroll and wait"},
            {id: "send_comms", text: "Send comms"},
            {id: "final", text: "Final"},
            {id: "fail", text: "Fail", className: styles.failNode},
        ],
        [
            {id: "1", from: "start", to: "enroll_and_wait"},
            {id: "2", from: "enroll_and_wait", to: "fail"},
            {id: "3", from: "enroll_and_wait", to: "wait"},
            {id: "4", from: "wait", to: "send_comms"},
            {id: "5", from: "send_comms", to: "final"},
            {id: "6", from: "send_comms", to: "fail"},
        ],
    ];
}, [formData]);

<Canvas
  nodes={graphNodes}
  edges={graphEdges}
/>{noformat}



Per-node styling is apparently possible via CSS styling. No pre-made styles (e.g. rounded edges, internal node structure, etc.). Entire graph styling is possible by providing custom Node/Edge components that will apply to *_all_* _nodes and edges_. Some pre-made styles available for that.



Rendered graph is basically an SVG component in a fixed dimension box. It is possible to use custom components for nodes, but this entails managing SVG pieces and juggling {{foreignObject}}s. [Example project |https://github.com/Vadorequest/rwa-faunadb-reaflow-nextjs-magic/tree/with-local-storage?tab=readme-ov-file]experimenting with fully customized components.



Very basic controls for graph layout out of the box. Project uses Eclipse’s ELK library for the graph layout, but project homepage (with docs to further layout customization) is dead.


Latest reaflow version has some issues with admin-panel’s react 17. Fixable, but need to tweak webpack/lattice configs.



Project has 26 collaborators, but overall not very active, 1-3 commits per month for the last year.

